# Apache Flink DataStream API Basics

## Overview
Apache Flink is a distributed stream processing framework that allows you to process unbounded and bounded data streams. The DataStream API is the core API for stream processing in Flink.

## Core Concepts

### StreamExecutionEnvironment
The entry point for creating Flink streaming applications.

```java
StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
```

### DataStream
Represents a stream of data elements. Streams can be created from sources and transformed using operators.

## Reading CSV Files

### Creating a Custom Source Function
For reading CSV files (especially compressed ones), you can create a custom `SourceFunction`:

```java
public class GzipCsvSource<T> implements SourceFunction<T> {
    private final String filePath;
    private final Function<String, T> parseFunction;
    private volatile boolean isRunning = true;

    public GzipCsvSource(String filePath, Function<String, T> parseFunction) {
        this.filePath = filePath;
        this.parseFunction = parseFunction;
    }

    @Override
    public void run(SourceContext<T> ctx) throws Exception {
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(
                    new GZIPInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))))) {
            String line;
            while (isRunning && (line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                T record = parseFunction.apply(line);
                ctx.collect(record);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel() {
        isRunning = false;
    }
}
```

### Using the Source
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> stream = env.addSource(
    new GzipCsvSource<>(inputPath, MyEvent::fromCsv)
);
```

## POJO Requirements

Flink POJOs (Plain Old Java Objects) must meet these requirements:
1. Public class
2. Public no-argument constructor
3. All fields either public or with getters/setters
4. Field types must be supported by Flink's serialization

Example POJO:
```java
public class MyEvent {
    public long timestamp;
    public String jobId;
    public int eventType;

    public MyEvent() {}

    public MyEvent(long timestamp, String jobId, int eventType) {
        this.timestamp = timestamp;
        this.jobId = jobId;
        this.eventType = eventType;
    }

    public static MyEvent fromCsv(String line) {
        String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);
        return new MyEvent(
            Long.parseLong(fields[0]),
            fields[1],
            Integer.parseInt(fields[2])
        );
    }
}
```

## Basic Transformations

### Filter
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> filtered = stream.filter(event -> event.eventType == 1);
```

### Map
```java
DataStream<String> mapped = stream.map(event -> event.jobId);
```

### FlatMap
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> flatMapped = stream.flatMap(
    new FlatMapFunction<MyEvent, MyEvent>() {
        @Override
        public void flatMap(MyEvent event, Collector<MyEvent> out) {
            if (event.eventType == 1) {
                out.collect(event);
            }
        }
    }
);
```

## Writing Output

### Text File Sink
```java
stream.writeAsText(outputPath).setParallelism(1);
```

### Custom Sink
```java
stream.addSink(new SinkFunction<MyEvent>() {
    @Override
    public void invoke(MyEvent value, Context context) throws Exception {
        // Custom output logic
        System.out.println(value);
    }
});
```

## Executing the Job
```java
env.execute("Job Name");
```

## Important Notes
- Flink jobs are lazy - they only execute when `env.execute()` is called
- Parallelism can be controlled at the environment or operator level
- Sources and sinks should handle exceptions properly

