# Flink Keyed Streams and Aggregations

## Overview
Keyed streams partition data by a key, allowing Flink to process events with the same key together. This is essential for windowing, aggregations, and stateful operations.

## Creating Keyed Streams

### Using keyBy with Lambda
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> stream = ...;
KeyedStream<MyEvent, String> keyed = stream.keyBy(event -> event.userId);
```

### Using keyBy with KeySelector
```java
public class MyKeySelector implements KeySelector<MyEvent, String> {
    @Override
    public String getKey(MyEvent event) {
        return event.userId;
    }
}

KeyedStream<MyEvent, String> keyed = stream.keyBy(new MyKeySelector());
```

### Multiple Key Fields
```java
// Using Tuple2 as composite key
keyed = stream.keyBy(event -> Tuple2.of(event.userId, event.sessionId));
```

## Aggregations on Keyed Streams

### Sum, Min, Max
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> summed = keyed.sum("value");
DataStream<MyEvent> maxed = keyed.max("value");
DataStream<MyEvent> minned = keyed.min("value");
```

### Reduce
Combine elements with the same key:
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> reduced = keyed.reduce(
    new ReduceFunction<MyEvent>() {
        @Override
        public MyEvent reduce(MyEvent event1, MyEvent event2) {
            return new MyEvent(
                event1.key,
                event1.count + event2.count
            );
        }
    }
);
```

### Aggregate
For more complex aggregations with different input and output types:
```java
DataStream<Result> aggregated = keyed.aggregate(
    new AggregateFunction<MyEvent, Accumulator, Result>() {
        @Override
        public Accumulator createAccumulator() {
            return new Accumulator(0, 0.0);
        }

        @Override
        public Accumulator add(MyEvent event, Accumulator acc) {
            return new Accumulator(
                acc.count + 1,
                acc.sum + event.value
            );
        }

        @Override
        public Result getResult(Accumulator acc) {
            return new Result(acc.count, acc.sum / acc.count);
        }

        @Override
        public Accumulator merge(Accumulator acc1, Accumulator acc2) {
            return new Accumulator(
                acc1.count + acc2.count,
                acc1.sum + acc2.sum
            );
        }
    }
);
```

## Stateful Operations

### MapState Example
```java
public class StatefulMapper extends RichMapFunction<MyEvent, Result> {
    private transient MapState<String, Long> state;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        MapStateDescriptor<String, Long> descriptor =
            new MapStateDescriptor<>("state", String.class, Long.class);
        state = getRuntimeContext().getMapState(descriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public Result map(MyEvent event) throws Exception {
        Long count = state.get(event.key);
        if (count == null) {
            count = 0L;
        }
        count++;
        state.put(event.key, count);
        return new Result(event.key, count);
    }
}
```

### ValueState Example
```java
public class CounterFunction extends RichFlatMapFunction<MyEvent, Result> {
    private transient ValueState<Long> counter;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        ValueStateDescriptor<Long> descriptor =
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("counter", Long.class);
        counter = getRuntimeContext().getState(descriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public void flatMap(MyEvent event, Collector<Result> out) throws Exception {
        Long currentCount = counter.value();
        if (currentCount == null) {
            currentCount = 0L;
        }
        currentCount++;
        counter.update(currentCount);
        out.collect(new Result(event.key, currentCount));
    }
}
```

## Working with Multiple Streams

### Union
Combine streams of the same type:
```java
DataStream<MyEvent> stream1 = ...;
DataStream<MyEvent> stream2 = ...;
DataStream<MyEvent> unioned = stream1.union(stream2, stream3);
```

### Connect
Combine two streams of different types:
```java
DataStream<Event1> stream1 = ...;
DataStream<Event2> stream2 = ...;

ConnectedStreams<Event1, Event2> connected = stream1.connect(stream2);

DataStream<Result> processed = connected.map(
    new CoMapFunction<Event1, Event2, Result>() {
        @Override
        public Result map1(Event1 value) {
            return new Result(value);
        }

        @Override
        public Result map2(Event2 value) {
            return new Result(value);
        }
    }
