# Flink Job Completion Detection and Filtering

## Overview
In streaming applications, you often need to detect when a job or entity has completed, and then filter or process based on completion status. This is common in cluster computing, workflow analysis, and event processing.

## Strategies for Completion Detection

### 1. Using Job Event Stream

When you have a separate stream of job lifecycle events (start, finish, fail), you can use these to filter or enrich task events.

```java
DataStream<JobEvent> jobEvents = ...;
DataStream<TaskEvent> taskEvents = ...;

// Filter for finished jobs
DataStream<JobEvent> finishedJobs = jobEvents
    .filter(event -> event.eventType == FINISH_EVENT_TYPE);
```

### 2. Broadcast State Pattern

Use broadcast state to maintain a set of completed jobs and filter task events:

```java
// Create broadcast descriptor
MapStateDescriptor<String, Boolean> jobStateDescriptor =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("finished-jobs", String.class, Boolean.class);

// Broadcast finished jobs
BroadcastStream<JobEvent> broadcastJobs = jobEvents
    .filter(event -> event.eventType == FINISH_EVENT_TYPE)
    .broadcast(jobStateDescriptor);

// Connect task stream with broadcast
DataStream<TaskEvent> filteredTasks = taskEvents
    .connect(broadcastJobs)
    .process(new BroadcastProcessFunction<TaskEvent, JobEvent, TaskEvent>() {
        @Override
        public void processElement(
                TaskEvent task,
                ReadOnlyContext ctx,
                Collector<TaskEvent> out) throws Exception {

            Boolean isFinished = ctx.getBroadcastState(jobStateDescriptor)
                .get(task.jobId);

            if (isFinished != null && isFinished) {
                out.collect(task);
            }
        }

        @Override
        public void processBroadcastElement(
                JobEvent job,
                Context ctx,
                Collector<TaskEvent> out) throws Exception {

            ctx.getBroadcastState(jobStateDescriptor)
                .put(job.jobId, true);
        }
    });
```

### 3. KeyedCoProcessFunction Pattern

Process both streams together with keyed state:

```java
// Key both streams by job ID
KeyedStream<TaskEvent, String> keyedTasks = taskEvents.keyBy(t -> t.jobId);
KeyedStream<JobEvent, String> keyedJobs = jobEvents.keyBy(j -> j.jobId);

// Connect and process together
DataStream<Result> results = keyedTasks
    .connect(keyedJobs)
    .process(new KeyedCoProcessFunction<String, TaskEvent, JobEvent, Result>() {

        private ValueState<Boolean> jobFinished;
        private ListState<TaskEvent> bufferedTasks;

        @Override
        public void open(Configuration parameters) {
            jobFinished = getRuntimeContext().getState(
                new ValueStateDescriptor<>("finished", Boolean.class));
            bufferedTasks = getRuntimeContext().getListState(
                new ListStateDescriptor<>("tasks", TaskEvent.class));
        }

        @Override
        public void processElement1(
                TaskEvent task,
                Context ctx,
                Collector<Result> out) throws Exception {

            // Check if job has finished
            Boolean finished = jobFinished.value();
            if (finished != null && finished) {
                // Job already finished, process task
                out.collect(new Result(task));
            } else {
                // Buffer task until job finishes
                bufferedTasks.add(task);
            }
        }

        @Override
        public void processElement2(
                JobEvent job,
                Context ctx,
                Collector<Result> out) throws Exception {

            if (job.eventType == FINISH_EVENT_TYPE) {
                // Mark job as finished
                jobFinished.update(true);

                // Process all buffered tasks
                for (TaskEvent task : bufferedTasks.get()) {
                    out.collect(new Result(task));
                }
                bufferedTasks.clear();
            }
        }
    });
```

### 4. Window-Based Completion

If you know jobs complete within a time window:

```java
// Collect all events for a job within a window
keyedTasks
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.hours(1)))
    .process(new ProcessWindowFunction<TaskEvent, Result, String, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void process(
                String jobId,
                Context ctx,
                Iterable<TaskEvent> tasks,
                Collector<Result> out) {

            // Window has closed, assume job is complete
            // Process all tasks
            long count = 0;
            for (TaskEvent task : tasks) {
                count++;
            }
            out.collect(new Result(jobId, count));
        }
    });
```

## Complete Example: Process Sessions Only for Finished Jobs

```java
// Step 1: Identify finished jobs
DataStream<String> finishedJobIds = jobEvents
    .filter(event -> event.eventType == 4) // FINISH event
    .map(event -> event.jobId);

// Step 2: Create broadcast state
MapStateDescriptor<String, Boolean> finishedJobsDescriptor =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("finished-jobs", String.class, Boolean.class);

BroadcastStream<String> broadcastFinishedJobs = finishedJobIds
    .map(jobId -> Tuple2.of(jobId, true))
    .broadcast(finishedJobsDescriptor);

// Step 3: Compute sessions for all jobs
DataStream<JobSession> allSessions = taskEvents
    .filter(event -> event.eventType == 0) // SUBMIT events
    .keyBy(event -> event.jobId)
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(new CountAggregator(), new SessionProcessor());

// Step 4: Filter sessions for finished jobs only
DataStream<JobSession> finishedJobSessions = allSessions
    .connect(broadcastFinishedJobs)
    .process(new BroadcastProcessFunction<JobSession, Tuple2<String, Boolean>, JobSession>() {
        @Override
        public void processElement(
                JobSession session,
                ReadOnlyContext ctx,
                Collector<JobSession> out) throws Exception {

            Boolean isFinished = ctx.getBroadcastState(finishedJobsDescriptor)
                .get(session.jobId);

            if (isFinished != null && isFinished) {
                out.collect(session);
            }
        }

        @Override
        public void processBroadcastElement(
                Tuple2<String, Boolean> value,
                Context ctx,
                Collector<JobSession> out) throws Exception {

            ctx.getBroadcastState(finishedJobsDescriptor)
                .put(value.f0, value.f1);
        }
    });

// Step 5: Find max session per job
DataStream<JobSession> maxSessions = finishedJobSessions
    .keyBy(session -> session.jobId)
    .reduce((s1, s2) -> s1.taskCount > s2.taskCount ? s1 : s2);
```

## Important Considerations

1. **Event Ordering**: Job finish event might arrive before all task events
2. **Buffering**: May need to buffer task events until job completion is known
3. **State Size**: Be mindful of state growth when buffering events
4. **Watermarks**: Ensure watermarks progress properly across connected streams
5. **Late Events**: Handle task events arriving after job finish event

